WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.642
A robot reaches for a block.

2
00:00:01.762 --> 00:00:03.230
The grasp looks reasonable.

3
00:00:03.350 --> 00:00:07.091
But a small misalignment can turn the next movement into a failed stack.

4
00:00:07.333 --> 00:00:09.989
When should it continue, and when should it ask for help?

5
00:00:10.233 --> 00:00:17.617
Equilibrium Energy Control, or EqC, looks inside action generation, then uses that
evidence to guide learning.

6
00:00:17.934 --> 00:00:22.895
A generative policy repeatedly refines noise into a chunk of future actions.

7
00:00:23.015 --> 00:00:26.071
Think of the entire chunk as one point in action space.

8
00:00:26.300 --> 00:00:30.361
These optimization steps are computations, not robot movements.

9
00:00:30.481 --> 00:00:33.238
The robot then executes only a short prefix.

10
00:00:33.467 --> 00:00:35.157
It observes again and replans.

11
00:00:35.277 --> 00:00:37.340
Each replan leaves a refinement trace.

12
00:00:37.460 --> 00:00:40.208
Could that trace reveal weakly supported behavior?

13
00:00:40.533 --> 00:00:43.216
EqC makes a scalar energy explicit.

14
00:00:43.336 --> 00:00:48.890
Images, instructions, and robot state condition a landscape over candidate action
chunks.

15
00:00:49.133 --> 00:00:52.238
Here, higher energy means greater learned compatibility.

16
00:00:52.358 --> 00:00:56.158
Gradient ascent refines noise toward an approximate equilibrium.

17
00:00:56.400 --> 00:01:00.498
The field depends on action and context, without explicit diffusion time.

18
00:01:00.618 --> 00:01:04.974
Under smoothness and suitable step sizes, updates increase the learned energy.

19
00:01:05.300 --> 00:01:07.977
Why should this landscape lead toward useful actions?

20
00:01:08.097 --> 00:01:12.363
Training connects noise to an expert action with a straight interpolation bridge.

21
00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:18.965
Along this bridge, the energy gradient learns the direction toward the
demonstration, scaled by a known schedule.

22
00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:21.663
The schedule vanishes at the expert endpoint.

23
00:01:21.783 --> 00:01:25.981
Bridge time selects training targets; the deployed field does not require it.

24
00:01:26.333 --> 00:01:28.559
Local slopes contain more than a direction.

25
00:01:28.679 --> 00:01:33.634
Accumulate their contributions from an intermediate bridge point to the expert
endpoint.

26
00:01:33.866 --> 00:01:40.601
With exact gradient matching, integration gives squared bridge length times the
remaining schedule integral, C of t.

27
00:01:40.833 --> 00:01:43.683
Normalizing gives a reference energy-to-go curve.

28
00:01:43.803 --> 00:01:47.530
Training supplies both a direction and a relationship we can check.

29
00:01:47.867 --> 00:01:50.215
Why not use final energy as confidence?

30
00:01:50.335 --> 00:01:52.919
Shift the whole landscape upward by a constant.

31
00:01:53.039 --> 00:01:55.201
Actions and gradients stay unchanged.

32
00:01:55.433 --> 00:01:58.800
Absolute energy changes, despite identical behavior.

33
00:01:58.920 --> 00:02:00.942
Energy differences cancel the offset.

34
00:02:01.167 --> 00:02:04.530
And plausible endpoints can hide different refinement histories.

35
00:02:04.650 --> 00:02:08.126
EqC checks the pattern of energy progress along the path.

36
00:02:08.467 --> 00:02:10.949
At deployment, the expert action is unknown.

37
00:02:11.069 --> 00:02:16.278
EqC uses its final candidate as an approximate equilibrium, a self anchor.

38
00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:18.444
Connect starting noise to that endpoint.

39
00:02:18.564 --> 00:02:22.333
Project intermediate iterates onto this chord to measure progress.

40
00:02:22.567 --> 00:02:27.818
Normalize remaining energy change by squared chord length, with a small floor for
short chords.

41
00:02:27.938 --> 00:02:29.622
Now compare it with the reference.

42
00:02:29.934 --> 00:02:32.659
Compare observed energy-to-go with the reference curve.

43
00:02:32.779 --> 00:02:35.542
Each vertical gap is a consistency residual.

44
00:02:35.767 --> 00:02:42.017
Take absolute values and average across refinement states: the energy-consistency
score, U delta E.

45
00:02:42.137 --> 00:02:45.556
Absolute values prevent gaps at different states from cancelling.

46
00:02:45.800 --> 00:02:49.453
Small scores indicate agreement; large scores flag mismatch.

47
00:02:49.573 --> 00:02:54.530
Computing the score requires neither an extra uncertainty network nor expert
actions.

48
00:02:54.833 --> 00:02:58.560
This tests self-consistency, not calibrated failure probability.

49
00:02:58.680 --> 00:03:02.357
A wrong but internally coherent trajectory can still score low.

50
00:03:02.600 --> 00:03:09.540
Theory connects the residual to integrated field mismatch, with corrections for
off-chord motion and normalization.

51
00:03:09.660 --> 00:03:11.527
It does not guarantee safe actions.

52
00:03:11.767 --> 00:03:18.065
The paper therefore evaluates actual task outcomes, and requires persistent alarms
before requesting help.

53
00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:19.806
Now assemble the system.

54
00:03:19.926 --> 00:03:25.877
Images, language, and robot state enter a diffusion transformer or
vision-language-action backbone.

55
00:03:26.100 --> 00:03:28.957
A differentiable scalar supplies energy gradients.

56
00:03:29.077 --> 00:03:33.874
Refinement proposes an action chunk, while its trace supplies the consistency score.

57
00:03:34.100 --> 00:03:37.576
Unless the alarm fires, execute a prefix and observe again.

58
00:03:37.696 --> 00:03:41.532
Action generation and introspection share one energy interface.

59
00:03:41.834 --> 00:03:43.685
One unusual score can be noise.

60
00:03:43.805 --> 00:03:48.208
Energy-Gated DAgger waits for consecutive replans above a threshold.

61
00:03:48.434 --> 00:03:51.399
A single spike advances the counter, then it resets.

62
00:03:51.519 --> 00:03:55.241
Persistent crossings fill the counter and trigger expert takeover.

63
00:03:55.467 --> 00:03:59.062
Remember the two clocks: inner refinement states create one score.

64
00:03:59.182 --> 00:04:03.256
Consecutive scores across physical replans determine intervention.

65
00:04:03.600 --> 00:04:05.945
The expert supplies a recovery trajectory.

66
00:04:06.065 --> 00:04:11.141
Add it to existing demonstrations, concentrating new examples where support was
weak.

67
00:04:11.366 --> 00:04:14.699
After the deployment round, train on the aggregated dataset.

68
00:04:14.819 --> 00:04:18.604
Weights change during this later update, not at the moment of intervention.

69
00:04:18.833 --> 00:04:23.436
On the next deployment, previously assisted situations may become autonomous.

70
00:04:23.556 --> 00:04:26.661
Repeated recovery and updates can expand competence.

71
00:04:26.967 --> 00:04:31.494
We conduct a range of experiments to demonstrate the performance of our method.

72
00:04:31.638 --> 00:04:36.283
We test three tasks: debris sweeping, flower insertion, and towel folding.

73
00:04:36.427 --> 00:04:44.501
Each task compares Galaxea G0.5 with its EqC augmented version, using twenty trials per method.

74
00:04:45.133 --> 00:04:49.692
On debris sweeping, success rises from sixty-five to one hundred percent.

75
00:04:49.836 --> 00:04:55.161
The rates use twenty trials per task; this is one illustrative EqC rollout.

76
00:04:58.833 --> 00:05:04.755
For flower insertion, the same comparison improves success from forty-five to eighty percent.

77
00:05:04.899 --> 00:05:08.196
EqC grasps, reorients, and inserts the flower.

78
00:05:11.967 --> 00:05:15.498
Towel folding improves from eighty to ninety-five percent.

79
00:05:15.642 --> 00:05:22.504
Across the three tasks, average success rises from sixty-three point three to ninety-one point seven percent.

80
00:05:28.533 --> 00:05:32.936
Reliable failure detection is essential for issuing the right alarm.

81
00:05:33.080 --> 00:05:36.734
It tells the robot when to stop and wait for expert takeover.

82
00:05:37.400 --> 00:05:42.687
On pooled LIBERO and LIBERO-Plus rollouts, failure-detection AUROC rises

83
00:05:42.807 --> 00:05:45.650
from zero point eight four three for Diff DAgger

84
00:05:45.770 --> 00:05:48.255
to zero point nine two four for EqC.

85
00:05:48.567 --> 00:05:53.926
Using Diff DAgger detection on the EqC controller gives zero point eight eight nine.

86
00:05:54.046 --> 00:05:56.311
The path readout adds useful information.

87
00:05:56.600 --> 00:05:59.206
These measure ranking, not success probability.

88
00:05:59.326 --> 00:06:05.329
Separate operating thresholds use ten percent of rollouts for calibration, then
ninety percent for testing.

89
00:06:05.667 --> 00:06:08.247
Here is the same loop on the real robot.

90
00:06:08.391 --> 00:06:11.376
The energy-consistency signal tracks block stacking.

91
00:06:15.107 --> 00:06:19.032
Persistent mismatch triggers a failure alarm, and the robot pauses.

92
00:06:19.467 --> 00:06:21.919
The expert takes over and finishes the stack.

93
00:06:22.063 --> 00:06:24.762
The recovery becomes data for the next update.

94
00:06:33.867 --> 00:06:43.259
Thirty recoveries across five rounds raise mean autonomous success to ninety-six point seven percent, ahead of both
matched-budget baselines.

95
00:06:45.867 --> 00:06:48.873
Garment folding uses the same intervention rule.

96
00:06:49.017 --> 00:06:51.745
The robot and energy plot share one timeline.

97
00:06:54.507 --> 00:06:58.023
EqC continues while the path remains consistent.

98
00:06:58.167 --> 00:07:02.426
Persistent violations identify a state where recovery is needed.

99
00:07:08.267 --> 00:07:12.641
The alarm pauses execution; expert takeover completes the fold.

100
00:07:15.767 --> 00:07:24.114
Twelve recoveries raise garment folding from thirty to ninety-eight point three percent mean success across three
independent runs.

101
00:07:24.258 --> 00:07:28.883
EqC outperforms both Diff-DAgger and human-triggered intervention.

102
00:07:29.533 --> 00:07:32.089
Can the recovery expert also be automated?

103
00:07:32.233 --> 00:07:39.434
Astra starts from the same eighteen garment-folding demonstrations used to train the initial EqC policy.

104
00:07:39.578 --> 00:07:46.275
It fits a control script, then refines its code and reusable skills through full-task and takeover practice.

105
00:07:46.933 --> 00:07:54.396
When EqC raises an alarm, Astra observes the partially folded garment and adapts its script to the current state.

106
00:07:54.540 --> 00:07:56.837
The script then completes the recovery.

107
00:08:04.467 --> 00:08:08.208
Six Astra recoveries join the eighteen seed demonstrations.

108
00:08:08.352 --> 00:08:12.606
After training, EqC succeeds in all twenty autonomous tests.

109
00:08:12.750 --> 00:08:21.145
Once the expert is prepared, failure detection, takeover, data collection, and policy learning form a fully automated
pipeline.

110
00:08:21.289 --> 00:08:31.868
It distills the large model's control and spatial perception capabilities into executable skills, then into the compact policy
through recovery demonstrations.

111
00:08:32.534 --> 00:08:34.080
Return to the opening stack.

112
00:08:34.200 --> 00:08:40.608
A useful robot needs a plausible action, evidence about when to yield control, and a
way to learn from help.

113
00:08:40.834 --> 00:08:43.619
EqC connects these through one energy landscape:

114
00:08:43.739 --> 00:08:49.091
refine an action, inspect its refinement path, and turn recoveries into the next
learning round.

115
00:08:49.334 --> 00:08:54.651
This offers a practical route toward robots whose autonomous competence grows with
experience.
